Row Echelon Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119819 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119819 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0fd5a675-c692-505e-900b-d7500fb7c653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120050 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120050 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120050 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120050 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120050 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120050 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120050 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding a Basis of the Null Space of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120050 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120050 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120050 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120050 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "711b406b-35cb-5fdc-b1b0-d63789565aba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120394 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120394 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35529" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120394 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120394 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that  $\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{3}+2{x}_{4} \\\\ 2{x}_{3}-5{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{3},{x}_{4}\\in R}$, find a basis for $\\text{Null}(A).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120394 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40c213d6-56ad-5332-a71d-4be318f6c925" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120394 #edb/ref 1074 "Any vector in $\\text{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{3}+2{x}_{4} \\\\ 2{x}_{3}-5{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3},{x}_{4}\\in R.\n$$\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{3}+2{x}_{4} \\\\ 2{x}_{3}-5{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{4} \\\\ -5{x}_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore a basis for $\\text{Null}(A)$ is\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120690 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120690 #edb/ref 1061 17508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120690 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6cd915cb-1be5-57c4-9174-958e51421e23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120690 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120691 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120691 #edb/ref 1061 17509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120691 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f601bb80-6906-5220-b7c7-22b3ad93f773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120691 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120692 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120692 #edb/ref 1061 17510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120692 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a4d6c4c-5b51-5273-8870-97758393eeff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120692 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120693 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120693 #edb/ref 1061 17511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120693 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f391ffe0-615c-5d48-86e2-243cac8338e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120693 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120694 #edb/ref 1061 17512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120694 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "65f64dc2-9935-58c4-a4d2-93ee03872a6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120694 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121041 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that the null space $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ of a matrix $A$ consists of the solutions to the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}.$ To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the matrix ![](images/70/702794c0129df672cc7c4cc65260c9bfa7e33804ea9f71bba048cf62142a0a18.svg) Computing the reduced row echelon form of $A,$ we get\n\n![](images/16/16d52c068c616e2eca149a8e81929a98c85726361d7ea2291ab314fe7567e4ce.svg)\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system\n\n![](images/bb/bb8743bd8eb072476af0e9e31c0215916beeeb82fc371348de1e6013b38810f8.svg)\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x_1$ and $\\color{blue}x_2$ are basic variables (corresponding to the pivot columns) and $\\color{red}x_3$ is a free variable (corresponding to the non-pivot column).\n\nTherefore, any vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n![](images/6e/6e124e2c030f6cad72932d09211935b438f1189322ded40683a1f18618e35f3e.svg)\n\nSo, the set ![](images/24/2449d6abf866e2baa123e81c1d610940930c75c6b45ecf818d36c62191ef605f.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ generates the null space of $A.$\n\n**Note:** It might be the case that $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ for a particular matrix $A$ equals the so-called zero subspace $\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}$ containing only the zero-vector. The basis of $\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}$ is said to be the *empty set*, which is a set that does not contain any vectors. The empty set is denoted by $\\emptyset$ or $\\{\\,\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121041 #edb